                      "attribution": "Financial Times",
                      "pub_date": null,
                      "snippet": "Unitree, the world’s leading producer of humanoid robots, is preparing for a high-profile IPO on Shanghai’s Star Market in the latter half of 2026. Founded in 2016 by Wang Xingxing, the company has gained recognition for its advanced androids showcased in entertainment displays and public performances. Unitree shipped 5,500 humanoid robots in 2025, with revenue mainly derived from university and research customers, followed by consumer and industrial sectors. It boasts a gross margin of 59.8% and posted Rmb600 million in adjusted net profits on Rmb1.7 billion in revenues.\n\nThe IPO is expected to value Unitree at Rmb40 billion ($5.9 billion), reflecting investors’ optimism for expanded commercial applications of humanoid robots. However, analysts point out that only 9% of Unitree’s sales currently serve “real-world” industrial roles, raising concerns about long-term sustainability. Much of its success has been fueled by government-backed entertainment and research initiatives. Despite its manufacturing efficiency and profitability, experts argue Unitree must evolve into a solutions provider with scalable industrial applications to justify its lofty valuation. The company plans to invest nearly half of the Rmb2 billion it aims to raise in AI-driven robotics software to address this challenge.",
                      "title": "Will investors embrace China's humanoid robot champion?",
                      "url": "https://www.ft.com/content/721e3bed-285b-46d4-8151-8cf28cb5ef50?utm_source=chatgpt.com"
